.\" Manual page for dmrd; the full description is docs/dmrd.md.
.\" Source: src/dmrd.c
.TH "DMRD" "8" "November 24, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
dmrd \- decode ETSI DMR channels from radiod, vote between repeaters and follow trunked calls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmrd
.RI "[options] [-I input ...] [-R output] [-f sites] [input ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
dmrd listens to one or more multicast groups carrying complex baseband (I/Q)
channels from radiod and decodes every DMR (ETSI TS 102 361) channel on them,
both timeslots, each channel in its own thread: conventional repeaters and
networks, and Motorola Capacity Plus, Connect Plus and Capacity Max, Tier III
and Hytera systems. It logs every transmission with its talkgroup or unit,
source, colour code and encryption state, every control message (CSBKs,
multi-block CSBKs, short link control) with what it says about the site, and
packet data (headers, and the location, text, registration and short data
applications).
.PP
The speech of clear (unencrypted) calls can be sent as 8 kHz RTP audio streams
that monitor plays and pcmrecord records (when dmrd is built with mbelib). A
group call's stream is its talkgroup; when several repeaters carry the same
call, as a multi-site system's do, dmrd makes one stream of it and takes each
20 ms of speech from whichever repeater received it best.
.PP
With a sites file (\-f) dmrd knows which repeaters make up each site. When a
site announces a call, or its rest channel, on a repeater no channel carries,
dmrd asks radiod for that repeater for as long as it is needed.
.PP
This page is a summary. The package's docs/dmrd.md describes the radiod
configuration, the sites file, call following, voting, the encryption rule and
the log lines in full.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-I group, \-\-input group"
Add an input group. May be repeated; positional groups are also accepted.
.TP
.B "\-R group, \-\-pcm\-out group"
Send decoded speech to this multicast group. Without it no audio is sent.
Needs a dmrd built with mbelib.
.TP
.B "\-N name, \-\-name name"
Name the voice output is advertised under. Default: not advertised.
.TP
.B "\-T ttl, \-\-ttl ttl"
Multicast TTL of the voice output. Default 0: this host only.
.TP
.B "\-p tos, \-\-tos tos"
IP type of service of the voice output. Default 184 (expedited forwarding).
.TP
.B "\-f file, \-\-sites file"
The sites file: one section per site, naming its system, type, colour code,
repeater frequencies and their logical channel numbers. A channel joins the
site that lists its frequency, whichever radiod sends it; any other channel is
a site of its own. See SITES.
.TP
.B "\-y type, \-\-system\-type type"
The kind of system a channel no site lists belongs to: conventional (the
default), tier3, capacity_plus, linked_capacity_plus, connect_plus,
capacity_max, hytera_xpt or hytera_tier3.
.TP
.B "\-c n|any|track, \-\-color\-code n|any|track"
The colour code to decode, 0\-15. track (the default) takes each channel's from
its first strong sync and follows it; any decodes every one.
.TP
.B "\-s bs|ms|direct|all|auto, \-\-stations bs|ms|direct|all|auto"
Which stations' syncs to look for: bs (the default), a repeater output; ms, a
repeater input or simplex; direct, TDMA direct mode; all, every family; auto,
every family until one family's synced bursts lead each other's by more than
10, then that one only.
.TP
.B "\-M, \-\-mobile"
The same as \-s all.
.TP
.B "\-L, \-\-learn\-crc\-masks"
Accept messages whose check fails with a residual seen twice for the same
message type within two minutes (systems such as Motorola's restricted access
to system, RAS). A link control word so accepted never says a call is clear.
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-no\-follow"
Do not ask radiod for repeaters: decode only the channels already there, and
do not probe a dark site.
.TP
.B "\-H seconds, \-\-hold seconds"
Keep a followed repeater this long after the last sign of its call. Default 5.
.TP
.B "\-g list, \-\-talkgroups list"
Follow group calls on only these talkgroups, for example 1,3,9000\-9099. May be
repeated. A call whose talkgroup is not known yet, and a unit-to-unit call, is
followed.
.TP
.B "\-x list, \-\-exclude list"
Never follow group calls on these talkgroups. May be repeated.
.TP
.B "\-m n, \-\-max\-calls n"
Follow at most n calls at once. Rest channels and probes do not count.
Default: no limit.
.TP
.B "\-S ssrc, \-\-ssrc ssrc"
Decode only this channel. Turns call following off.
.TP
.B "\-o source, \-\-source source"
Accept only this sender, by name or address (source-specific multicast).
.TP
.B "\-A interface, \-\-iface interface"
Set default multicast interface.
.TP
.B "\-t seconds, \-\-timeout seconds"
Close a channel after this long without data. Default 20.
.TP
.B "\-u seconds, \-\-update seconds"
Interval between the status lines and site summaries that \-v turns on.
Default 10.
.TP
.B "\-l locale, \-\-locale locale"
Locale for number formatting. Default $LANG.
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Increase diagnostics; repeat for more. At \-v each channel also logs a status
line and each site a summary, and link control, talker aliases and probes of a
dark site are logged.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH RADIOD CONFIGURATION
dmrd needs I/Q, not demodulated audio, at 19.2 kHz (four samples per 4800 baud
symbol), with the AGC off and a fixed gain. Configure every repeater output to
decode; for a trunked site, at least its rest or control channel. Give the I/Q
its own group, one nothing else listens to. An example is in
config/examples/fragments/dmr.conf:
.PP
.nf
[DMR]
data = dmr\-iq.local
mode = iq
samprate = 19200
low = \-6250
high = +6250
encoding = f32le
agc = no
gain = 60
freq = "453m4250 453m9750 460m1375 460m6375"
.fi
.PP
A DMR repeater's signal is often strong and the band busy: fix the front end's
gain below its overload point (on an Airspy R2, gainstep = 17 with
agc\-low\-threshold = \-100 in its section). Overload shows as a channel with
signal and no syncs.
.PP
Repeaters dmrd asks for are asked for in the form of the I/Q channels it
already decodes from that radiod, with a lifetime of \-H seconds, refreshed
while they are needed; radiod builds them from its [global] section, whose
"data =" must be set. Where there is more than one radiod, each is asked in
turn until one covers the frequency.
.SH SITES
.nf
[SCMTD 1]
system = SCMTD
site = 1
type = capacity_plus
color\-code = 0
freq = "453m4250 460m1375"
lcn = "1 2"
.fi
.PP
system and site name the site in the logs and the streams' descriptions
(default: the section's name); type and color\-code are as \-y and \-c; freq
lists the site's repeater outputs and lcn their logical channel numbers, in the
same order (none for a conventional site). learn\-crc\-masks is as \-L for the
site, and compressed\-talkgroups = yes says that the system's talkgroups are in
the dialable form of TS 102 361\-2 annex C (Hytera Tier III), so that a group
call's talkgroup is converted to the number a user dials before it is used.
See config/examples/dmrd\-sites.conf.
.PP
A Capacity Plus site's rest channel, when it is on a repeater no channel
carries, is asked for and kept while the site names it. When every channel
dmrd decodes for a site of more than one repeater has been silent for 5
seconds, its other repeaters are probed, 2 seconds each, until one carries
bursts.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
Decoded events go to standard output, one line each, starting with the time of
the signal, the SSRC and the channel frequency, or the site's name for a line
about a site; errors and startup messages go to standard error. A control
message repeated many times is written once and then counted.
.PP
The voice stream (\-R) is 8 kHz mono 16-bit audio, one RTP packet per 20 ms,
with status packets as radiod sends them. A group call's stream has the
talkgroup as its SSRC, whichever repeater carries it; any other call's, the
channel's SSRC times ten plus the timeslot. The status carries the frequency
and signal figures of the repeater the speech came from and a description
such as "SCMTD TG 3 src 1301 453.4250 MHz TS2 CC0".
.PP
Encrypted voice is not decoded: a transmission's voice is decoded only after a
checked header or embedded link control word of that transmission has said it
is unencrypted. Voice that arrives before that is held, then sent in order or
dropped.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGUSR1 increases verbosity; SIGUSR2 decreases it. SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGQUIT
stop dmrd; transmissions in progress are ended and the repeaters it asked for
are released.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B "What has been tested."
On the air, from this tree's radiod with an Airspy R2: a Motorola Capacity Plus
system of three sites (wide-area calls, rest channel moves, both slots), with a
sites file and without, call and rest channel following on it, and 67 amateur
repeater outputs at once. From recordings and generated signals: each layer,
frame for frame and field for field, and the codes against the tables of TS
102 361\-1 annex B. Not yet on the air: Tier III, Connect Plus, Capacity Max or
Hytera signalling, a site with more than one repeater, packet data, and more
than one radiod.
.TP
.B "The vocoder is optional."
Speech decoding uses mbelib, as p25d does; see p25d(8) for what it does and
does not do, and its README's caution about patents.
.TP
.B "Talkgroup numbers are unique only within one system."
Run one dmrd, with its own output group, per system, or accept that two
systems' calls on one number are voted as one (they do not line up, and the
second gets its own stream).
.SH CREDITS
sdrtrunk (Dennis Sheirer, GPL-3) and OP25 (Max H. Parke KA1RBI, Graham J.
Norbury and others, GPL-3) were used as references for DMR implementation
details, along with ETSI TS 102 361. Taken from them, with a credit beside each
in the source: the sync words, the code tables and layouts, the CRC masks, the
link control, talker alias, CSBK and packet data layouts, and the rules for
learning CRC masks and choosing the station family automatically. Speech
decoding uses mbelib (https://github.com/szechyjs/mbelib) when it is installed.
.PP
Much of what is here was first worked out, prototyped and run in production by
the author in the
receivers\ behind\ scanrad.io.
That is especially true of the front-end signal processing: the approach to
receive filtering, carrier frequency tracking, symbol timing recovery and
burst framing was originally written for those receivers and tuned there.
Those receivers are not ka9q-radio code: what is here is that work, rewritten
by the author in plain C for this tree with the assistance of Anthropic's
Claude (the Fable and Opus models).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B dmrd@.service
systemd template unit; the instance name is the input group. Its output is
appended to /var/log/dmrd.log.
.TP
.B config/examples/fragments/dmr.conf
Example radiod section.
.TP
.B config/examples/dmrd-sites.conf
Example sites file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode a group and send the speech of clear calls:
.PP
.nf
dmrd \-R dmr\-pcm.local dmr\-iq.local
.fi
.PP
A trunked system described by a sites file, following its calls on talkgroups
1 to 99 only:
.PP
.nf
dmrd \-f /etc/ka9q\-radio/dmrd\-sites.conf \-g 1\-99 \-R dmr\-pcm.local dmr\-iq.local
.fi
.PP
As a service:
.PP
.nf
systemctl enable \-\-now dmrd@dmr\-iq.local
.fi
